# Export these variables in your shell; the Go binary does not load dotenv files.
# DATABASE_DSN_FILE or DATABASE_DSN is required; the DSN selects the database automatically.
# Put ONE of these examples in a private 0600-permission DSN file:
# MySQL: dnsmgr:EXAMPLE_PASSWORD@tcp(127.0.0.1:3306)/dnsmgr
# PostgreSQL: postgres://dnsmgr:EXAMPLE_PASSWORD@127.0.0.1:5432/dnsmgr?sslmode=disable
# PostgreSQL production: use sslmode=verify-full and a trusted CA.
# SQLite: sqlite:/absolute/path/to/dnsmgr.db (Windows: sqlite:C:/path/to/dnsmgr.db)
DATABASE_DSN_FILE=/absolute/path/to/private/database-dsn
SETUP_TOKEN_FILE=/absolute/path/to/private/setup-token
LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1:18081
PUBLIC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:18081
STATE_DIR=/absolute/path/to/private/state
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4
# Frontend assets are embedded in dnsmgr; build them with npm --prefix web run build before compiling Go.
# MASTER_KEY_FILE defaults to STATE_DIR/master.key; retain it with backups.
# SQLite needs no database server or C compiler; WAL, foreign keys and a 10-second busy timeout are enabled.
# SQLite uses BEGIN IMMEDIATE transactions and adjacent .locks/ OS file locks.
# API/Worker must share one canonical absolute SQLite path on local disk and one master key.
# Allow writes to the database, its -wal/-shm files and .locks/ directory; do not delete locks while running.
# SQLite is single-host only; use MySQL/PostgreSQL for multiple hosts.
